WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.025 --> 00:00:10.069 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to episode one of our U.S. states series. First, I clear the map so we
can study a fresh group.

2
00:00:10.733 --> 00:00:20.369 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
We begin with all six New England states, then New York, New Jersey and
Pennsylvania. Together they form the Census Northeast.

3
00:00:21.043 --> 00:00:28.160 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring New England closer. Watch the northeastern corner move into view,
with Maine reaching up toward Canada.

4
00:00:31.025 --> 00:00:40.325 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Maine is our first stop: the big northeastern state, beside Canada. New
Hampshire is its only neighbouring U.S. state.

5
00:00:40.981 --> 00:00:49.514 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The card offers two possible origins for Maine's name. One points to the
French province; another to sailors referring to the mainland.

6
00:00:50.178 --> 00:01:00.836 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its forest fact is memorable: close to nine-tenths of Maine's land is
forested. Picture a wooded coast at the top of our route.

7
00:01:04.074 --> 00:01:14.860 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Follow Maine's southwestern edge into New Hampshire. Notice the small stretch
that reaches the Atlantic; most of the state extends inland.

8
00:01:15.514 --> 00:01:26.903 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name comes from Hampshire in England. Another card fact recalls the Old
Man of the Mountain, a rock profile that collapsed in 2003.

9
00:01:27.564 --> 00:01:36.771 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Keep the neighbours straight: Maine to the east, Vermont to the west,
Massachusetts below. Next, we'll compare New Hampshire with Vermont.

10
00:01:39.082 --> 00:01:48.846 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Vermont is the inland state immediately west of New Hampshire. New York lies
to its west, Canada above it, and Massachusetts below.

11
00:01:49.497 --> 00:02:01.304 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name comes from French words meaning green mountain. The card recalls that
Vermont was an independent republic from 1777 to 1791.

12
00:02:01.965 --> 00:02:13.262 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
In 1791 it joined the United States as the fourteenth state. Remember the
northern pair: Vermont on the left, New Hampshire on the right.

13
00:02:13.947 --> 00:02:21.621 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's zoom closer to southern New England. Massachusetts and the small states
below it now have more room on the map.

14
00:02:23.958 --> 00:02:32.858 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Now Massachusetts, directly south of New Hampshire. Follow its eastern coast
around Boston and out toward the hook of Cape Cod.

15
00:02:33.524 --> 00:02:46.237 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The name comes from the Massachusett people. The card also mentions Harvard,
founded in 1636: the oldest institution of higher education in the United
States.

16
00:02:46.899 --> 00:02:55.038 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Use Massachusetts as our horizontal anchor. The two small states immediately
below it are Rhode Island and Connecticut.

17
00:02:57.315 --> 00:03:07.764 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Here's Rhode Island, the tiny state below Massachusetts and just east of
Connecticut. The whole state is more than the islands in its bay.

18
00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:20.913 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It's the smallest U.S. state by area. The card recalls its longer official
name, which included Providence Plantations until voters shortened it in 2020.

19
00:03:21.568 --> 00:03:31.506 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
For the map, remember the tiny coastal slot: Massachusetts above, Connecticut
to the left. Small state, but worth a careful look.

20
00:03:33.782 --> 00:03:44.057 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Slide west into Connecticut. It sits below Massachusetts, beside Rhode Island,
with Long Island Sound along its southern edge.

21
00:03:44.718 --> 00:03:55.213 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name is linked to an Indigenous expression for the long tidal river. That
gives us a landscape clue as well as a name to remember.

22
00:03:55.871 --> 00:04:07.725 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
That's all six New England states: Maine, New Hampshire, Vermont,
Massachusetts, Rhode Island and Connecticut. Next, we turn toward New York.

23
00:04:08.425 --> 00:04:19.129 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's move southwest to New York. Its inland reach, Long Island, and its
neighbours New Jersey and Pennsylvania now come into view.

24
00:04:21.499 --> 00:04:29.893 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
This broad state is New York. Most of it extends inland, while Long Island
projects into the Atlantic beside Connecticut.

25
00:04:30.541 --> 00:04:40.259 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The card takes us to the Erie Canal, opened in 1825. It connected the Hudson
River with the Great Lakes, making an inland route to the coast.

26
00:04:40.922 --> 00:04:49.838 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Think of New York as the bridge between the Great Lakes and the Atlantic. New
Jersey lies just below its southeastern corner.

27
00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:57.254 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's look more closely at New Jersey, between the Atlantic coast and
Pennsylvania across the Delaware River.

28
00:04:59.843 --> 00:05:09.143 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
New Jersey is the narrow coastal state south of New York. Pennsylvania is
inland to its west, across the Delaware River.

29
00:05:09.808 --> 00:05:20.640 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name comes from Jersey in the Channel Islands. Another fact brings us to
Edison's Menlo Park laboratory, where his phonograph was invented in 1877.

30
00:05:21.292 --> 00:05:30.116 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember New Jersey's narrow coast beside Pennsylvania. We turn inland to
Pennsylvania next, our final northeastern state.

31
00:05:30.799 --> 00:05:38.044 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Now widen the view westward to Pennsylvania, the final member of our Northeast
group.

32
00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:51.222 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Pennsylvania is the broad state south of New York and east of Ohio. Notice its
small northwestern corner reaching Lake Erie.

33
00:05:51.887 --> 00:06:02.382 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name means Penn's woods. In Philadelphia, Independence Hall was the
setting for the signing of the Declaration of Independence and the
Constitution.

34
00:06:03.032 --> 00:06:14.120 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Pennsylvania completes our nine northeastern states. Keep it below New York,
above Maryland, and west of New Jersey across the Delaware River.

35
00:06:19.053 --> 00:06:31.928 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
New England gives us Maine, New Hampshire, Vermont, Massachusetts, Rhode
Island and Connecticut. Then New York, New Jersey and Pennsylvania complete
our nine-state group.

36
00:06:32.576 --> 00:06:43.071 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Vermont west of New Hampshire, the small coastal pair below
Massachusetts, and Pennsylvania south of New York. Next time we follow the
Atlantic south.
